title: The baseline of a view
pid: 62464b75-220a-4e0b-986e-c1b86997ae4f
body: |2-
  A view's baseline is the text the server last drew for it, as the
  client installed it (without the focus and fold metadata the client
  removes), before any edit. Every response that gives the client a buffer to install as a
  view draws one: =content-view=, =search-results=, =diff-view=,
  =save-result=, =collateral-view= and =rerender-view=, the last two
  once per view. Each client keeps the baseline per buffer (Emacs in
  'skg-clean-baseline', Neovim in 'vim.b.skg_clean_baseline', the
  tool in the view's '.full.org' file), recaptures it whenever a
  response redraws the buffer, and sends it with the buffer's save as
  the envelope's =baseline= ("Save buffer"). A buffer no view response
  drew, such as a new empty view, has as its baseline the text the
  client created it with. The server compares the baseline with the
  graph to decide whether the save is stale ("Save buffer", the stale
  buffer); a dirty other view's baseline serves the dirty-view
  conflict check. The fork-confirmation buffer has none: it is not
  saved, and approving it re-saves the source buffer, with that
  buffer's baseline.
